    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d221">

                <pb facs="0706" n="692" sameAs="frus:frus1872p2v1/pg692"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hall, district attorney, to Mr. Clayton, Secretary
                        of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <dateline><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Southern District of New York,
                            United States District Attorney’s Office</hi></placeName>,<lb/><date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1850-05-25"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 25,
                        1850</date>.</dateline>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your letter of the 21th instant is just
                    received, and I have sent for the Spanish consul to call at this office
                    forthwith, and furnish such information as may enable me to proceed at once
                    under the sixth section <pb facs="0707" n="693" xml:id="pg693"/> of the act of
                    1818. You may rely upon “a rigid execution of the law,” so far as it can be
                    executed by your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>J. PRESCOTT HALL,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States
                            Attorney.</hi></signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">John M. Clayton</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State, &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                    <p>P. S.—Before your letter was received, I had addressed a communication to you
                        upon this subject, which you will doubtless receive tomorrow.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
